  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerTitle">Dangerous Action</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerBody">This change can break the system or weaken security. Check the box, then confirm. If you are not sure, click Cancel.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.dangerCheck">I understand the risks and am doing this on purpose</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.previewTitle">Preview Changes</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="dlg.blocked">This item is intentionally locked. Turning it off could break Windows; WinCleaner will not allow it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagtrack.name">Connected User Experiences (DiagTrack)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagtrack.desc">Sends Windows usage data to Microsoft. Turning it off cuts background reporting — most people notice nothing. It won’t magically raise FPS, but you get better privacy and less background noise.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dmwappush.name">Device Management WAP Push</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dmwappush.desc">Old-style push and device-management messaging, plus related telemetry. Most home users can turn it off and never notice.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wer.name">Windows Error Reporting</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wer.desc">Sends crash reports to Microsoft when a program fails. It doesn’t prevent crashes — Manual is usually enough if you don’t care about those reports.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblauth.name">Xbox Live Auth Manager</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblauth.desc">Handles sign-in for Xbox, Microsoft account games, and Game Pass. Only disable it if you never use Xbox or Game Pass — otherwise the Xbox app, some games, and friends list can break.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblgame.name">Xbox Live Game Save</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xblgame.desc">Syncs Xbox cloud saves. Local saves still work if you turn it off, but cloud sync may stop — be careful if you use Game Pass.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxnet.name">Xbox Live Networking</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxnet.desc">Keeps Xbox online features connected. Turning it off can affect multiplayer and Xbox social features.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxgip.name">Xbox Accessory Management</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.xboxgip.desc">Manages Xbox controllers and accessories. Leave it alone if you use a wireless Xbox controller.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.spooler.name">Print Spooler</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.spooler.desc">Sends print jobs to your printer. If you never print at home, turning it off frees a useless idle service — but printing and PDF print will stop working.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bthserv.name">Bluetooth Support</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bthserv.desc">Powers Bluetooth headphones, mice, and controllers. Only turn it off if you never use Bluetooth — otherwise Bluetooth dies completely.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.onesync.name">Sync Host (OneSync)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.onesync.desc">Syncs Mail, Calendar, Contacts, and similar accounts. Built-in Mail/Calendar sync may break if you disable it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.cdp.name">Connected Devices Platform</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.cdp.desc">Runs Nearby sharing and phone–PC bridge features. Games are fine without it; those sharing features just stop.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagsvc.name">Diagnostic Execution Service</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.diagsvc.desc">Runs Windows troubleshooters in the background. Manual means it only runs when needed, with less daily load.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dusm.name">Data Usage</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dusm.desc">Feeds the data-usage counter in Settings. Turning it off may break the counter, not your internet.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.sysmain.name">SysMain (Superfetch)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.sysmain.desc">Tries to predict and preload apps into memory. On SSDs that can mean busy disk for little gain — many gamers turn it off to calm 100% disk spikes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wsearch.name">Windows Search</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wsearch.desc">Builds a search index in the background and keeps poking the disk. Start search gets slower without it, but search still works and the disk rests.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.rpcss.name">Remote Procedure Call (RPC)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.rpcss.desc">Windows’s internal communication layer — almost everything depends on it. Disabling it breaks the system. WinCleaner locks this on purpose.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dcom.name">DCOM Server Process Launcher</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.dcom.desc">Lets programs call each other at a low level. Turning it off wrecks Windows. Locked on purpose.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.windefend.name">Windows Defender Antivirus</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.windefend.desc">Windows’s built-in antivirus. Don’t disable it here even if you install another antivirus — WinCleaner blocks that path on purpose.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wuauserv.name">Windows Update</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wuauserv.desc">Downloads and installs security updates. Stopping it means quiet days now and growing security holes later — only if you know what you’re doing.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bits.name">Background Intelligent Transfer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.bits.desc">Background downloads for Windows Update and some other apps. Turning it off can break updates and those downloads.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.pcasvc.name">Program Compatibility Assistant (PcaSvc)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.pcasvc.desc">Watches for program compatibility issues and reports them to Microsoft. Apps still run if you turn it off; the prompts may just stop.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.lfsvc.name">Geolocation Service (lfsvc)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.lfsvc.desc">Gives apps your location (pairs with the Deny Location tweak). Maps, weather, and other location features stop without it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.remoteregistry.name">Remote Registry</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.remoteregistry.desc">Lets other PCs on the network edit this machine’s registry remotely. Home users don’t need it — safer left off.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.ssdpsrv.name">SSDP Discovery</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.ssdpsrv.desc">Automatically finds UPnP devices on your network (smart TVs, printers, and so on). Internet still works if you turn it off; discovery may need to be manual.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.upnphost.name">UPnP Device Host</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.upnphost.desc">Hosts UPnP devices together with SSDP Discovery. Some network device sharing may stop if you disable it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.retaildemo.name">Retail Demo</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.retaildemo.desc">Store / demo-mode service for retail PCs. Useless at home — safe to disable.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wmpnetwork.name">Windows Media Player Network Sharing</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.wmpnetwork.desc">Shares Windows Media Player libraries over the home network (DLNA). Local playback is fine without it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.tabletinput.name">Touch Keyboard and Handwriting</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="svc.tabletinput.desc">Touch keyboard, pen, and handwriting. On a desktop or laptop without a touchscreen you can usually turn it off safely.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.3dviewer.name">3D Viewer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.3dviewer.desc">Views 3D model files. Most people never need it — safe to remove, and you can get it back from the Microsoft Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.mixedreality.name">Mixed Reality Portal</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.mixedreality.desc">Setup portal for VR / mixed reality headsets. Without a headset it’s just wasted space — safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxapp.name">Xbox Console Companion</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxapp.desc">The older Xbox companion app. Think twice if you use Game Pass — some social and Game Pass features may depend on it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxoverlay.name">Xbox Game Bar</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxoverlay.desc">The Win+G Game Bar overlay — recording, widgets, FPS counter. Removing it cuts background capture load and can free a bit of FPS; reinstall from the Store if you miss it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxidentity.name">Xbox Identity Provider</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.xboxidentity.desc">Identity provider for your Xbox account. Don’t remove it if you sign into Xbox services.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.skype.name">Skype</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.skype.desc">Classic Skype video chat. If you already use Discord or Teams, you can usually remove it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.solitaire.name">Solitaire Collection</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.solitaire.desc">Microsoft’s card and casual games pack. If you don’t play it, it’s just sitting on disk — safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.weather.name">Weather</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.weather.desc">Bing Weather app/widget. If you check weather on your phone, you don’t need it on the PC.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.news.name">News</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.news.desc">Bing News / headlines app. Remove it if you don’t want news shoved at you on the PC.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.officehub.name">Office Hub</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.officehub.desc">A “try / buy Office” promo hub. You don’t need it whether you own Office or not.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.people.name">People</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.people.desc">Contacts / address book app. Fine to remove if you don’t keep contacts there.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.feedback.name">Feedback Hub</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.feedback.desc">Sends feedback and suggestions to Microsoft. Safe to remove if you never open it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.gethelp.name">Get Help</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.gethelp.desc">Microsoft’s help chat and tips app. Skip it if you just search the web when something breaks.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.getstarted.name">Get Started</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.getstarted.desc">New-PC tips tour. After the first look you’ll never need it again — safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.maps.name">Maps</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.maps.desc">Windows Maps. If you use Google Maps or your phone, you can drop this.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunevideo.name">Films &amp; TV</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunevideo.desc">Microsoft’s Films &amp; TV player / store front. Fine to remove if you already use VLC or similar.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunemusic.name">Media Player (Groove)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.zunemusic.desc">The old Groove / music app. Unnecessary if you use Spotify or another player.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.clipchamp.name">Clipchamp</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.clipchamp.desc">Simple video editor bundled with Windows 11. Remove it if you don’t edit video and want the space back.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.teams.name">Microsoft Teams</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.teams.desc">Work/school meeting app that sometimes ships with Windows. Remove it if you don’t use it — leave it if work depends on it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.store.name">Microsoft Store</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.store.desc">The Microsoft Store — Xbox, many apps, and updates come through here. Removing it puts the system in a bad spot, so the checkbox is locked.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.photos.name">Photos</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.photos.desc">Default photo and video viewer. Optional if you already use another viewer.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.calculator.name">Calculator</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.calculator.desc">The Calculator app. Marked critical in the catalog so you can’t delete it by accident here.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.yourphone.name">Phone Link</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.yourphone.desc">Phone Link between your phone and PC. Safe to remove if you don’t use it; reinstall from the Store anytime.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.cortana.name">Cortana</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.cortana.desc">Voice assistant app — limited on modern Windows. Safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.widgets.name">Widgets (Web Experience)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.widgets.desc">Powers the taskbar Widgets panel. Removing it also removes the Widgets icon — some Win11 setups change taskbar behavior afterward.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.paint3d.name">Paint 3D</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.paint3d.desc">3D drawing app. Most people stick with classic Paint — safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.print3d.name">Print 3D</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.print3d.desc">3D printing app. Useless without a 3D printer — safe to remove.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.todo.name">Microsoft To Do</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.todo.desc">Microsoft To Do task list. Safe to remove if you use something else.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.quickassist.name">Quick Assist</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="app.quickassist.desc">Remote support app. Handy in rare support cases — remove only if you’re sure you won’t need it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamemode.name">Enable Game Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamemode.desc">Tells Windows you’re gaming so background updates and indexing step back and the game gets more focus. Harmless and recommended for gamers.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamebar.name">Disable Game Bar Capture</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamebar.desc">Turns off Game Bar’s always-ready background capture. Instant Win+Alt+G clips may stop working, but FPS usually improves — a favorite for competitive play.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamedvr.name">Disable Game DVR Policy</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.gamedvr.desc">Policy switch that disables Game DVR / background recording more aggressively. Turn it on if you don’t capture clips.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hags.name">Hardware-accelerated GPU Scheduling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hags.desc">Lets the GPU schedule frames with less CPU involvement (HAGS). Can lower latency or cause stutter on some driver/game combos — reboot after changing, and turn it off if things get worse.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ultimateperf.name">Ultimate Performance Power Plan</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ultimateperf.desc">Unlocks Windows’s hidden Ultimate Performance power plan so CPU/GPU save less power. FPS gets more consistent; laptops drain faster and the PC may run hotter.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nagle.name">Disable Nagle (TcpAckFrequency)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nagle.desc">Stops the network from bundling small packets before sending (TcpAckFrequency). Competitive online games may feel a bit snappier; rare networks might lose a little throughput.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpnodelay.name">TCP NoDelay</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpnodelay.desc">Same family as disabling Nagle — tells TCP to send immediately instead of waiting. Use it together with the Nagle tweak.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.fso.name">Disable Fullscreen Optimizations Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.fso.desc">Pushes games toward classic exclusive fullscreen instead of borderless-window behavior. Helps alt-tab and input in some older or picky titles — not required for every game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcss.name">MMCSS GPU Priority High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcss.desc">Raises GPU priority in Windows’s Games task profile so the graphics card gets more room while you play. Fine on most PCs (caution-level).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcssprio.name">MMCSS Priority High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcssprio.desc">Raises the scheduling priority number for games so Windows treats them as more important work.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcsssched.name">MMCSS Scheduling Category High</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mmcsssched.desc">Sets the games scheduling category to High. Works as a package with the other MMCSS tweaks.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bgapps.name">Restrict Background Apps</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bgapps.desc">Limits Store (UWP) apps from quietly running in the background. Less mystery CPU/RAM use; some notifications and sync may lag.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mouseaccel.name">Disable Enhance Pointer Precision</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.mouseaccel.desc">Turns off Enhance pointer precision (mouse acceleration) so aim distance matches hand movement. Desktop mouse feel changes at first — strongly recommended for FPS.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.vbs.name">Disable VBS / Memory Integrity</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.vbs.desc">Turns off Memory Integrity / core isolation (HVCI). Some PCs gain more consistent input or FPS, but kernel security gets weaker. Needs a reboot.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dynamictick.name">Disable Dynamic Tick</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dynamictick.desc">Sets disabledynamictick via bcdedit. May smooth DPC / frame times on some systems — not a miracle everywhere. Needs Administrator; reversible with bcdedit.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hpet.name">Disable HPET (useplatformclock)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hpet.desc">Removes forced platform clock usage (bcdedit /deletevalue useplatformclock). Helps latency on some boards, hurts others — measure before keeping it. Needs Administrator.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.telemetry.name">Minimize Telemetry</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.telemetry.desc">Sets Windows diagnostic data sent to Microsoft as low as possible. Less “Windows is watching” traffic; Home edition may not reach absolute zero but still cuts a lot.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.adid.name">Disable Advertising ID</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.adid.desc">Turns off the advertising ID apps use to track you. Less personalized ads; games keep working.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tailored.name">Disable Tailored Experiences</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tailored.desc">Stops tips, suggestions, and ads based on your diagnostic data — fewer “just for you” pop-ups.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.cortana.name">Disable Cortana Policy</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.cortana.desc">Disables Cortana via policy (already weak on newer Windows). Less voice-assistant background activity.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bing.name">Disable Bing Search in Start</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.bing.desc">Stops Start search from pulling Bing / web results so you only see what’s on your PC — faster and cleaner. A very popular tweak.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.location.name">Deny Location Access</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.location.desc">Generally denies apps asking for your location. Better privacy; maps and weather may lose location features.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.activity.name">Disable Activity History</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.activity.desc">Stops sending “which app you opened when” history to the cloud / Timeline. Cross-device resume gets weaker.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.visualperf.name">Best Performance Visual Effects</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.visualperf.desc">Tells Windows to prefer speed over polish — fewer shadows, smooth scrolls, and animations. The desktop looks flatter but feels lighter.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.animations.name">Disable Window Animations</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.animations.desc">Turns off minimize / maximize window animations so windows appear instantly.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.transparency.name">Disable Transparency</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.transparency.desc">Turns off frosted-glass transparency on the taskbar and Start. Slightly more matte look, a bit less GPU/CPU on eye candy.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.qos.name">Remove QoS Bandwidth Reserve</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.qos.desc">Clears Windows’s default bandwidth reserve (often ~20%). In theory the full pipe is yours — harmless to try even if the gain is small.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpauto.name">TCP Autotuning Normal</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.tcpauto.desc">Sets TCP window autotuning to normal via netsh. Useful when downloads or the network behave oddly — a one-shot command.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dnsflush.name">Flush DNS Cache</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.dnsflush.desc">Clears the PC’s cached DNS answers. Handy when a site won’t load, goes to the wrong place, or acts weird after a VPN — harmless one-time cleanup.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netbios.name">Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netbios.desc">Disables NetBIOS on every NetBT interface. Less legacy broadcast chatter on modern networks; very old LAN sharing may be affected.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.deliveryopt.name">Disable Delivery Optimization P2P</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.deliveryopt.desc">Stops Windows Update from sharing updates P2P with other PCs. Updates still come from Microsoft; your upload isn’t wasted — great on a data cap.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.wupause.name">Disable Windows Update Service</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.wupause.desc">Fully stops the Windows Update service. No surprise updates, but also no security patches — OK for a short pause before a tournament or stream, then turn it back on. Needs “allow dangerous actions”.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskceip.name">Disable CEIP Consolidator Task</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskceip.desc">Disables the Customer Experience Improvement scheduled task that sends usage summaries to Microsoft. One less telemetry job.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskusbceip.name">Disable USB CEIP Task</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskusbceip.desc">Disables the CEIP task that tracks USB device usage. Less telemetry; USB devices still work.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.classicmenu.name">Classic Context Menu</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.classicmenu.desc">Restores the full classic right-click menu on Windows 11 instead of the compact one. Explorer restarts briefly when you toggle it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.copilot.name">Disable Windows Copilot</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.copilot.desc">Turns off Windows Copilot via policy (taskbar / Win+C). Windows 11 only.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.recall.name">Disable Windows Recall</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.recall.desc">Disables Windows Recall / AI screen-data analysis via policy. Recommended for privacy on Windows 11.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.startiris.name">Hide Start Recommendations</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.startiris.desc">Turns off Start menu “recommended / Iris” content for a cleaner Start. Explorer may restart.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.widgets.name">Disable Widgets / News Feed</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.widgets.desc">Disables taskbar Widgets and the News and interests feed. Less background chatter; Explorer may restart.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.inking.name">Disable Inking &amp; Typing Personalization</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.inking.desc">Stops sending keyboard / handwriting usage data to Microsoft for personalization. Suggestions get more generic; privacy improves.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.feedbackfreq.name">Silence Feedback Frequency</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.feedbackfreq.desc">Stops Windows “Give us feedback” survey pop-ups.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.onlinespeech.name">Disable Online Speech Recognition</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.onlinespeech.desc">Stops sending voice data to the cloud for speech recognition. Online speech features turn off; local ones stay.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.powerthrottle.name">Disable Power Throttling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.powerthrottle.desc">Stops Windows from throttling CPU for background / inactive windows. Minimized helpers like Discord get throttled less while gaming; laptop battery may suffer.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.sysresp.name">Multimedia System Responsiveness</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.sysresp.desc">Sets the CPU reserve for background tasks (default ~20%) to zero so games and multimedia get more CPU time.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.coreparking.name">Disable CPU Core Parking</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.coreparking.desc">Keeps all CPU cores awake on the current power plan (no core parking). Fewer lags on sudden load spikes; higher power use.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.darkmode.name">Enable Dark Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.darkmode.desc">Turns on dark theme for apps and the system UI.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.lockscreenads.name">Disable Lock Screen Ads / Tips</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.lockscreenads.desc">Turns off Windows Spotlight ads and tip overlays on the lock screen so you get a plain static image.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.showext.name">Show File Extensions &amp; Hidden Files</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.showext.desc">Shows file extensions and hidden files in Explorer — easier to spot fake “photo.jpg.exe” files. Explorer restarts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskbarleft.name">Align Taskbar Left</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.taskbarleft.desc">Moves Windows 11 taskbar icons back to the classic left alignment. Explorer restarts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidechat.name">Hide Taskbar Chat Icon</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidechat.desc">Hides the Teams / Chat icon on the taskbar. Explorer restarts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.searchicon.name">Shrink Search Box to Icon</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.searchicon.desc">Shrinks the wide taskbar search box to a small icon. Explorer restarts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidetaskview.name">Hide Task View Button</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.hidetaskview.desc">Hides the Task View button on the taskbar without uninstalling anything. Explorer restarts.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.explorerthispc.name">Open Explorer to This PC</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.explorerthispc.desc">Makes File Explorer open to This PC instead of Home.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netthrottle.name">Reset Network Throttling Index</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.netthrottle.desc">Removes Windows’s multimedia/game network packet processing limit. Under heavy online traffic, packet delay may drop.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ipv6.name">Disable IPv6</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.ipv6.desc">Disables IPv6 system-wide. Some modern services (console crossplay, certain VPNs) may need IPv6 — don’t combine with Prefer IPv4. Use only if IPv6 is causing connectivity issues; reboot recommended.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nicpower.name">Disable NIC Power Saving</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="tweak.nicpower.desc">Stops Windows from sleeping network adapters to save power. Can reduce random micro-disconnects and lag spikes in online games.</sys:String>
